title: $:/plugins/huwiki/forgeshare/advanced-en
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

!! 1. Prepare a server wiki

* Back up your HTML wiki first. Keep the original as an offline copy.
* Install Node.js 20 or later, TiddlyWiki (tested with 5.4.1), and Tailscale from their official sources. Sign in to Tailscale on the host and iPad/other clients. The helper does not install software or change your Tailscale login.
* Create a ''new'' server folder with TiddlyWiki's standard command:

```sh
tiddlywiki MySharedWiki --init server
```

Start locally first, import a copy of your Forge HTML using TiddlyWiki's Import, review the list, and select your content/plugins. Do not replace the server core or connection plugins. Wait for saves to complete, stop the server, and restart after importing plugins.

```sh
tiddlywiki MySharedWiki --listen host=127.0.0.1 port=8080
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:8080` yourself. This preparation is local only; without authentication other local users can read and write. The server needs `tiddlywiki/tiddlyweb` and `tiddlywiki/filesystem`; the helper requires them in `tiddlywiki.info`.

!! 2. Technical start without dialogs

<a href={{$:/plugins/huwiki/forgeshare/download/script}} download="ForgeShare.mjs">Download standalone ForgeShare.mjs</a>

Run `node ForgeShare.mjs` for terminal mode or `node ForgeShare.mjs --gui` for dialogs. Terminal mode needs an existing private credentials CSV, installs nothing and requires typing `START` after the summary. Dialog mode can install TiddlyWiki 5.4.1 locally after separate approval, uses a temporary login and stops when the status dialog closes. Neither mode opens a browser.

!! 3. Authentication and safe sharing

Create a private UTF-8 CSV file ''outside the wiki folder'', with header `username,password` and one user per line. Use unique passwords of at least 12 characters. The simple helper does not accept commas, quotation marks or line breaks in values. On Mac/Linux the file must be owned by you and accessible only to you (`chmod 600`). On Windows, restrict its file permissions to your account; the helper cannot verify Windows ACLs. Never store the file in the wiki, Git or a shared folder.

The helper grants read and write access to every account in that file. For different permissions, start manually using TiddlyWiki's `readers`/`writers`. Tailnet access and wiki authentication are separate controls. Restrict Tailscale grants/ACLs to the intended people and port ''10000'', especially when sharing the host: sharing can otherwise expose other services too. The helper does not change grants/ACLs.

|! Service |! Tailscale → local port |
| Existing AI | 443 → 11434 (preserved) |
| Existing LM Studio/AI | 8443 → 1234 (preserved) |
| ForgeShare | 10000 → 8080 |

The helper checks the existing Serve configuration, refuses an occupied 10000 and starts only an available local 8080 bound to `127.0.0.1`. It never uses `serve reset`, Funnel, `--bg`, or an implicit port-443 command. Do not change Serve concurrently in another terminal. The targeted command is:

```sh
tailscale serve --https=10000 http://127.0.0.1:8080
```

Tailscale needs HTTPS/MagicDNS configured; follow its official setup if Serve refuses to start. When the helper reports ready, open the displayed `https://machine.tailnet.ts.net:10000` yourself on both devices. Share the address only with intended users and send credentials through a separate private channel. An address alone does not grant tailnet access. ''Serve'' is private within the tailnet; ''Funnel'' exposes a service to the internet and is not used here.

!! 4. Sync speed

The Mac browser is a separate client even when the Mac hosts the server. Receiving clients poll for updates; saving, network delays and background-tab suspension also affect timing.

Confirmed user test: Mac and iPad synced both ways over Tailscale. With `$:/config/SyncPollingInterval` set to `3000` and a reload, perceived speed became roughly equal. This is an observation, not a promise of a maximum three-second delay.

Current setting (ms): <$text text={{{ [{$:/config/SyncPollingInterval}else[60000]] }}}/>. Without an override, tested TW 5.4.1 uses 60000 ms. Shorter intervals increase requests.

<$list filter="[{$:/status/IsReadOnly}!match[yes]]" variable="ignore">
<$button><$action-setfield $tiddler="$:/config/SyncPollingInterval" text="3000"/>Use 3000 ms in this wiki</$button>
</$list>

The button changes only this setting after your click; it starts no service. A server wiki may share the setting with other clients. Save, wait for sync to finish, then reload ''every'' client. Save drafts first. To undo, restore your previous value in [[$:/config/SyncPollingInterval]] or delete that setting tiddler for the core default. Installing ForgeShare does not change polling.

!! 5. Troubleshooting

* ''Local address fails:'' check Node.js/TiddlyWiki, the wiki folder, terminal errors and port 8080. The helper never takes over or kills an existing server.
* ''Local works, iPad fails:'' check Tailscale on both devices, HTTPS/MagicDNS, access rules and the complete address including `:10000`. Check `tailscale status` and `tailscale serve status`. Keep the host awake.
* ''401/login error:'' check the CSV header, password and username. ''403/read-only:'' check `readers`/`writers`. Do not disable CSRF protection to work around an error.
* ''Mac receives slowly:'' check the polling value and reload the Mac browser too. Keep tabs active when measuring; iPad may suspend background tabs.
* ''Missing changes:'' check both browsers use the same server address, not a local HTML copy. Inspect TiddlyWiki sync status/logs. Export unsaved tiddlers before reloading after errors.
* ''Port 10000 is occupied:'' inspect Serve status. The helper refuses to replace existing configuration. Do not reset everything; establish who owns the service first.

!! 6. Backup, conflicts and offline use

TiddlyWiki is not a simultaneous text editor with automatic merging. Agree who edits each tiddler; concurrent edits can overwrite each other. If a conflict occurs, stop editing, export both versions under different titles, and compare manually. Faster polling does not solve conflicts.

Sync is not backup: mistakes and deletions spread too. Wait for saves, stop the server and copy the entire server folder to a dated backup. Keep multiple versions and test restoration in a ''new'' folder. Store credentials separately and privately. The helper does not make automatic backups.

Use TiddlyWiki's built-in ''Download save''/HTML export to keep a standalone offline copy. Test opening and saving it with the server stopped. Back up external attachments separately. Offline edits do not automatically sync back: compare and import selected tiddlers after backing up. ForgeShare replaces no saver and needs no server to display this guide.

